  "intro": {
    "text": "Programs make decisions by asking yes/no questions. A **comparison** asks one and gives back `True` or `False`. These are values of type **bool** (capital T and F, no quotes).\n\n- `==` equal to, `!=` not equal to\n- `<` less than, `>` greater than\n- `<=` at most, `>=` at least\n\nOne `=` stores a value. Two `==` compare. You can chain comparisons: `1 < x < 5` asks if x is between 1 and 5.\n\nText is compared letter by letter. Capitals come before lowercase letters, and `\"Bo\" == \"bo\"` is False.",
    "analogy": "A comparison is like a bouncer at a door with one rule, such as \"at least 18\". He looks at your age and answers only yes or no. True and False are those two answers, written down so you can use them later."
  },
